WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.297 --> 00:00:01.380
CHRIS WILCOX: Hey, Martin.

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:02.463
You brought a map.

00:00:02.463 --> 00:00:03.630
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah, Chris.

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:06.430
I just came back from a hike,
which I logged in the app

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:07.500
Take a Hike.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.350
They built a system for
logging your hikes online.

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:12.900
It is serverless, and
exposes data from a Cloud SQL

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.010
database as a REST API.

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:16.510
CHRIS WILCOX: In a
previous episode,

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:19.320
we showed how one can
build serverless REST APIs.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.900
In this episode, we'll
see how they made sure

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.840
that each user can only
access their own logged hikes.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.200
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:34.813 --> 00:00:36.230
MARTIN OMANDER:
Ana is a developer

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:40.430
working for Take a Hike, a
service for logging your hikes.

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:44.240
She built a REST API to enable
users to track their activity

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:45.965
from a web and mobile app.

00:00:45.965 --> 00:00:48.090
CHRIS WILCOX: It turns out
it's quite easy to build

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:49.770
a REST API in Cloud Run.

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:52.190
As a bonus, Cloud
Run is serverless,

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:54.340
so there are no
servers to manage.

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:58.370
And Take a Hike will only pay
per-call with no fixed costs.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:00.830
MARTIN OMANDER: Ana built
the REST API in Cloud Run

00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:02.520
as a proof of concept.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.850
Now that it's working well,
she needs to address security.

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:08.780
A user should only be
able to view and edit

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:12.265
their own hikes, not
those of other users.

00:01:12.265 --> 00:01:13.640
CHRIS WILCOX:
Ana's first thought

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:15.890
is that the server would
send the user's ID to the web

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:16.610
client.

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:19.040
When that user logs
in, the web client

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.290
would then send the user's
ID back to the server

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:23.180
when accessing the REST API.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:25.680
MARTIN OMANDER: But
there is a problem.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:29.060
Ana remembers that one
cannot trust code running

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:30.310
on the client.

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:32.990
A malicious user could
modify the client code

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:35.330
so it sends a different
user ID than the one it

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:37.070
received from the server.

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:38.440
We're back where we started--

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:41.865
any client can read
any recorded hike.

00:01:41.865 --> 00:01:44.240
CHRIS WILCOX: Ana does some
research and finds technology

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:45.350
that might help--

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:48.470
JSON Web Tokens, or JWTs.

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:51.050
When the client app logs
in, it gets the user ID

00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:53.762
from the server
signed by the server.

00:01:53.762 --> 00:01:55.220
MARTIN OMANDER:
The client app then

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:58.220
attaches the JSON
Web Token to any REST

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:00.960
call it makes to the server.

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:04.100
But Ana wonders, is there a
way to stop a malicious user

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:06.290
from modifying this token?

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:08.240
CHRIS WILCOX: As part
of the JWT standard,

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.880
the server signs all
tokens digitally.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:12.740
If the client app
modifies the token

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:14.780
and sends it back to
the server, the server

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:17.800
will know that it was tampered
with and can refuse the call.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:19.550
MARTIN OMANDER: This
means that Ana's code

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:22.040
that runs on the server
can trust the user

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:24.080
ID it gets from the client.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:26.900
She can use that user
ID in her SQL code

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:29.675
when looking up the
user's recorded hikes.

00:02:29.675 --> 00:02:31.550
CHRIS WILCOX: This sounds
great in principle,

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:34.550
but would it be hard for
Ana to write this code?

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:37.245
She doesn't have any
experience with cryptography.

00:02:37.245 --> 00:02:38.120
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah.

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:40.340
Whenever you write
security-related code

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:44.390
like this, it's best to lean
on well-tested libraries.

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:46.340
If Ana does that,
there's less risk

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:48.680
of leaving a security hole open.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:52.100
Fortunately, she doesn't have
to create the JSON Web Token.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.210
Google's servers do that.

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:56.510
And when she decodes
the token, she

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:59.000
can use a library
provided by Google.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.010
CHRIS WILCOX: This
is really cool.

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:02.620
What does the code look like?

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:04.370
MARTIN OMANDER: Tune
into a future episode

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:07.010
and we'll see Ana's code.

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:10.010
CHRIS WILCOX: Hey, Martin,
where do you hike to?

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:13.910
MARTIN OMANDER: Uh, I walked
over to the refrigerator.

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:15.290
CHRIS WILCOX:
Well, I suppose you

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:17.210
can take a long hike next time.

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:20.560
[MUSIC PLAYING]

